          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Instruction broadly denotes divine teaching, revealed guidance, Torah, and formative moral training, with discipline or correction as a common contextual sense.\",\"book_summary\":\"Instruction is specifically the law’s corrective discipline, characterized by its acceptance among the wise and rejection by fools.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Divine teaching or formative guidance, including Torah and corrective discipline.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The corrective discipline of the law.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book restricts instruction to law-based discipline and correction, excluding or leaving unaddressed broader cases such as general scriptural teaching, parental instruction, apostolic doctrine, and non-corrective divine guidance. It also foregrounds reception by the wise versus rejection by fools.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book selects a recognized Torah-and-discipline sense within the consensus rather than replacing the consensus’s central category.\",\"rationale\":\"The consensus explicitly includes both Torah and discipline/correction as established senses of instruction. The book combines and narrows these to the law’s corrective discipline, so its referent remains within the same general category but has a materially smaller extension.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Proverbs 1:7 and 23:23 clearly connect instruction with wisdom and with fools’ rejection or the wise person’s valuation, but the excerpts do not themselves explicitly identify instruction as the law’s discipline and correction. That specification depends on lexical or broader contextual interpretation.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
